# Source-only paired assembly

`evaluation.run_paired(packet, snapshot_root, out, *, transports, caps, prompts, models, include_full_index=False)` assembles fake/replay arms. This source has not been executed. Runtime validation is unverified; comparative acceptance is incomplete; human-adjudicated evaluation is not measured. The required live extraction and single-pass experiments remain outstanding.

`packet` is the closed `EvaluationPacket`. `transports`, `prompts` and `models` each have exactly two keys: `extraction` and `single_pass`. Values are D4's built-in `FakeTransport`/`ReplayTransport`, explicit prompt SHA256 digests and declared model labels respectively. `caps` is the central `ProviderCaps`. There are no labels, gold, rationale, candidate claims or target tool arguments in the request API. New exact-span atoms do not need prior discovery by the rules inventory.

The runner seals canonical packet bytes once. The candidate, independent literal/path comparator, extraction and single-pass roles share the same packet and `sha256(canonical_json(packet))`. The packet's internal digest separately hashes its fields excluding `packet_digest`. Extraction and single-pass receive newly decoded copies of those same bytes through `providers.produce(request, *, transport, ledger)`. Prompt, arm, model, policy and caps are separately request-bound. Output directories must be new and outside the snapshot; no prior result is overwritten.

Each provider role gets its own ledger with the same declared total input/output/time/cost caps. A retry shares its role's total budget, rather than gaining a fresh cap. Single-pass permits one logical request. The producer owns reservation-before-attempt and settlement; unknown settlement blocks further attempts. `Attempt` observations remain distinct from `SimulatedUsage`. Fixture usage is never presented as actual provider tokens, latency or measured live cost. Built-in transport admission refuses live execution; no SDK, credential read or network adapter is added here.

The runner screens the README and every admitted evidence excerpt. The producer also screens the complete request. A held arm retains an `abstention_screened` prediction row with no physical attempts. Invalid proposals, input failures, citation failures, incomplete settlements, failed physical retries and final provider failures remain rows. Every response and attempt is recorded, including a failed retry followed by a successful proposal. The full candidate inventory, blocks and errors are retained in `inventory.json`; extraction omissions are not replaced with candidate atoms. Gold-aware omission denominators are established only by later scoring, never by inference.

The packet grants no full-index query or absence certificate. Frozen full-file reads are limited to reopening README/admitted excerpt pointers for integrity. The sole `citations.validate_span` validates all claim and model evidence pointers, even unused proposed pointers. An invalid pointer becomes a citation failure. A quoted excerpt's presence proves its bytes, not semantic support.

The shared `packet_facts` authority now establishes exact path presence and scoped declarations/literals using complete files wholly present in admitted excerpts. It reuses the existing trusted isolated AST/TOML parser primitive (`index._parser_packet`); it never constructs a `StaticIndex`, invokes full-index resolution, reads undisclosed source or issues query coverage. Partial excerpts remain named gaps. Python namespace resolution requires an explicit unambiguous file/module, and ambiguous/dynamic/shadowed/conditional contexts abstain. TOML facts require the complete admitted `pyproject.toml`. Named bounded-code patterns, literal environment references, dependency declarations, metadata and CLI mappings use the same authority for candidate and model treatments. An opposite literal can accuse only an exact unique complete binding; a different key, module, function or value fragment cannot stand in for it. Null/boolean/numeric/string values remain type-exact.

Every proposed source span is independently parsed with the bounded grammar, then checked against complete raw assertion spans derived from Markdown blocks. This context list contains no rule predicates or candidate claims. A narrow quote cannot erase a surrounding condition, negation or example fence. New exact-span atoms may establish packet facts without appearing in the rules inventory; unsupported or ambiguous atoms remain prediction rows with unknown findings. `audit.policy.decide_finding` owns the four-way reduction. No absent excerpt or grep miss proves absence. Positive declaration/path evidence can refute an exact negated assertion, and an opposite whole literal in its unique complete scope can contradict the expected value; these use positive/contrary spans, never invented negative certificates. Proposed relations/pointers remain in the persisted response and never dictate verdicts.

Original byte offsets, quotes and hashes are authoritative. Packet evidence display lines use `RawDocument`'s LF/CRLF/bare-CR line table. Python input to the isolated parser uses tokenizer-style universal newline normalization; resulting UTF-8 byte columns map back through the original and normalized `RawDocument.line_bytes`. TOML retains its original syntax bytes and full-file spans use original coordinates. No Python string offset substitutes for a byte offset. Parser enforcement failures or unmappable/partial contexts remain gaps/unknowns rather than an unbounded fallback.

`include_full_index=True` requests the separate `product_full_index` utility treatment through the existing rules audit and report verifier. Its findings/errors have their own prediction rows; its input hash is explicitly absent from equal-packet metadata. Its complete audit artifacts stay under `full-index-run/`. Packet/model arms never receive its index, coverage, findings or additional source. Do not pool it with the equal-packet results.

The output includes `packet.json`, `packet-facts.json` after valid input, `inventory.json` when available, an arm directory with `predictions.jsonl`, `evidence.json` and `ARM.json`, durable provider budget/event directories, and `PAIRED.json`. Budget directories are named `live_extraction-budget` and `live_single_pass-budget`, containing `budget.json`, `requests.json` and `attempts.jsonl` when produced. Their raw hashes belong to the paired artifact closure. `PAIRED.json` pins source-code/parser/fact-policy versions and publishes state atomically using exclusive temporary writes, flush/fsync and replace. Successful source assembly closes as `closed_source_only`; a packet failure closes as `failed_input` with a row for every requested arm. A write failure may leave an unfinished directory; preserve it and use a fresh output, never interpret a missing close record as success. `comparative_complete` and `human_adjudicated` always remain false.

`verify_paired(run_root: Path, snapshot_root: Path, *, manifest_digest: str | None = None) -> dict` is the offline paired verifier. Its return keys are `manifest` (dict), `manifest_digest` (SHA256 of raw `PAIRED.json`), `packet` (`EvaluationPacket`), `predictions` (per-arm lists of `PredictionRow`) and `attempts` (per-arm lists of `Attempt`). It rejects unclosed/old-version bundles, missing or extra artifacts, noncanonical/duplicate-key JSON, hidden attempts/ledger files, wrong packet/request/claim/row IDs, dropped rows and incompatible proof relabeling. It checks admitted pointers with the sole citation service and replays packet-owned facts/policy; it never executes a provider. Full-index utility is checked by the separate existing report verifier. Input artifacts are bounded to 32 MiB each and 64 MiB total; row files are bounded to 20,000 including explicit pipeline failures. A bundle that cannot replay in the exact source/parser environment is not silently accepted.

The optional externally recorded `manifest_digest` pins a bundle. Without it the result proves self-consistency and semantic replay only, not origin/authorship or actual execution. Failure rows attest the recorded failure; structural verification cannot prove a transient error occurred. `AuditError` reasons include `paired_not_closed`, `paired_artifact_integrity`, `paired_identity_mismatch`, `paired_row_integrity`, `paired_attempt_integrity`, `paired_proof_mismatch` and `paired_invalid_artifact`; sole-citation errors remain intact. Ordinary storage errors may propagate. A recorded failed input with invalid source identity remains a failure record, not a scorable verified input.

`score_paired(run_root: Path, snapshot_root: Path, gold_path: Path, out: Path, *, manifest_digest: str | None = None) -> dict[str, EvaluationResult]` verifies before opening gold. Gold must describe this one snapshot and exactly one unambiguous curator family, otherwise it raises `paired_gold_scope_mismatch`/`ambiguous_family`. Exact gold spans reopen through the sole citation resolver, and `SCORED.json` binds the same raw gold bytes that were scored. It remaps failure rows as well as findings, retaining both all-gold omission denominators and all-attempt failure rows. Output must be a new directory outside the closed run and source; existing output raises `output_exists`, nested output raises `output_inside_paired_run`/`output_inside_snapshot`. Per-arm `RESULTS.json` and `items.json` plus `SCORED.json` are descriptive fake/replay outputs, with both acceptance flags false and no single-packet family interval. Provider summaries retain missing observations; simulated usage is not actual token/latency/cost measurement. No scoring has occurred in this source-construction run.

For later activation, an operator constructs packet-bound `ProviderFixture` instances (including explicit simulated usage and request digest), supplies built-in fixture transports and calls `run_paired` from reviewed source. D4 owns a future CLI entrypoint; no shell command is claimed runnable until that entrypoint is published. A replay fixture must be bound to the exact arm/prompt/model/policy/caps request, with one explicit fixture per retry. No invocation was performed during Dirty Mode.

