The R03 source adds `run_live_experiment` with the exact signature documented in [evaluation](evaluation.md). It retains `run_paired` as the explicitly fake/replay API. Both use one internal packet/artifact engine and the same fact, row-assembly, verifier and scorer authorities. No second HTTP, provider or accounting layer is introduced. `prepare_dev_packet(snapshot_root, out)` gives a genuine unadmitted DEV preparation using the same packet builder, without a fake admitted LOCK or fabricated receipts.

Shared artifact version `paired-source/3` records an explicit `experiment` context and `execution_summary`. A legacy fixture-port run has `experiment=null`, `mode=fake_or_replay_only` and `closed_source_only`. A locked experiment has an externally pinned raw `protocol-copy.json`, exact `prompts/extraction.txt`/`prompts/single_pass.txt`, closed owner authorizations and `mode=locked_live_or_fixture`. It closes as `closed_fixture_unaccepted` or `closed_live_unaccepted`, reflecting declared transport provenance rather than benchmark acceptance. `execution_summary` counts recorded live versus replay/fixture attempts; held roles retain `not_attempted` and zero physical records. `source_only=true` describes the source-only inference input; it never means a later live call was unexecuted. The source/templates in this repository remain dormant and unexecuted now.

Provider arm metadata discriminates `fixture_port` from `live_protocol`. The former has separately labeled `simulated_usage`; the latter uses `LiveProviderResponse.usage` with explicit `fixture` versus `provider_reported` origin and nullable token/cost/time observations. Fixture observations never become actual live observations, and missing actual cost never becomes zero. Live ledger reservations bind authorization/payload identities, physical timestamps, logical request/treatment caps and the terminal response record. The verifier checks these records against exact request/prompt/model/policy/source identities and replays code-owned rows. It does not contact providers, read credentials, replay HTTP, establish actual network effects, authenticate external receipts or verify human custody. An externally pinned, independently observed execution remains necessary for those claims.

Incomplete/unsettled provider proposals remain exact-span unknown predictions plus explicit physical/final failure rows. A preparation or payload-admission error after admitted source control leaves `failed-before-inference/<arm>.jsonl` and `FAILED.json` with all required-arm failure denominators and rethrows the original error. It does not fabricate an `EvaluationPacket` or a closed passing paired run. These failure receipts are unscorable inputs for later corpus failure aggregation; the paired verifier refuses a nonexistent/invalid packet. Earlier missing/disabled authorization remains a refusal before source/output activity, not a fabricated experiment attempt.

For a locked single-packet score, `gold_seal_digest` binds the exact raw gold JSONL bytes supplied after closure. A different label file raises `paired_gold_seal_mismatch`. An aggregate corpus-manifest seal needs a separately specified aggregate adapter; this function does not silently reinterpret one or omit a failed family. Descriptive outputs distinguish fixture from live-unaccepted provenance, and both human/comparative acceptance flags remain false. Required corpus-wide comparisons, failed-preparation aggregation, real custody controls and actual measurements remain open until separately implemented/admitted and exercised after Dirty Mode.

Unknown producer usage/cost settlement stops later live-protocol dispatch across the experiment, including fixture injection that exercises that policy. The following role has no producer call or new reservation: its outcome is an explicit `experiment_unsettled_liability` failure, status incomplete, execution `not_attempted`. `dispatch_halt` and the recorded attempt summary must replay in the one verifier, which rejects a later response after unresolved liability. Real HTTP mode requires the optional API's `ledger_root` to be supplied as a durable owner store outside source/output. The same protocol/packet/role store is reused when output directories change; pre-existing pending/unknown state refuses dispatch until reconciled. Closed artifact copies preserve the producer journal/state without changing or resetting accounting. Shared-store concurrency, reconciliation/resumption and storage-failure behavior still require actual normal-mode validation.

For a multi-case dataset, the dataset driver must verify every case closure before opening any case gold. Each case's protocol digest pins its own LOCK and raw gold JSONL seal. The dataset's independently retained manifest digest/common study headers are a different identity; never substitute one case's gold seal for aggregate admission. The single-case scorer remains the underlying primitive and cannot certify corpus-wide closure, blindness or human review on its own.

Closed full-index report scoring also uses the verified manifest snapshot to assign the unique curator family to every row, including failures without findings. All supplied gold and findings must share that snapshot; empty or ambiguous curator mappings refuse scoring. Verification still precedes gold access.


## Durable case entry and reconciliation

`run_live_experiment` supports first entry only for each owner-bound protocol/packet/role case. Before binding admission or admitting provider payloads, it inspects both role stores. Pending or unknown budget state refuses with `unsettled_liability`. Otherwise an existing `budget.json`, `requests.json` or `attempts.jsonl` marker in either role refuses with `case_continuation_not_supported`, including an empty journal or a fully settled/reconciled state. Spare logical capacity, a different prompt/request digest and a fresh output directory grant no replay authority. This check only reads existing producer files; it never initializes, resets or repairs a ledger. Invalid or unreadable state refuses through the existing validation/storage error instead of authorizing dispatch.

After a failed or interrupted future experiment, preserve the original output, durable admission, both role histories and actual receipt bytes. An authorized owner first inspects and reconciles pending liability through `providers.experiment.reconcile_configured_budget(*, owner_context, config, role, record, usage_receipt_data, pricing_receipt_data)` against the same admitted case. That service derives the exact admitted root/protocol/packet/role leaf and delegates receipt/state validation to `BudgetLedger.reconcile`; the runner does not reconcile or select an arbitrary recovery leaf. Reconciliation settles recorded liability; it does not finish an interrupted comparison, recover missing artifacts, authorize a replacement request or make an old attempt fresh. Re-entering this runner after reconciliation still refuses used history. No artifact-preserving continuation or automatic resume is implemented. Do not delete history, switch roots, alter packet/protocol identities or mint new admission merely to bypass the refusal. Any genuinely new experiment needs separate explicit owner authority and a reviewed protocol; this API grants none.

A closed original bundle can be independently verified/scored through the existing APIs; an unfinished output stays unfinished. A new rejected entry may contain preparation and all-required-arm `failed-before-inference` failure receipts, but creates no new provider attempt and no scorable closed paired run. Preserve both old and rejected-entry artifacts. The entry check is a sequential preflight, not an atomic multi-launcher claim; concurrent launches of the same case are unsupported and require owner serialization. No concurrency or recovery operation has been exercised in Dirty Mode.
